# G037 — P2P File Sharing App

*v2. See `p2p-file-sharing.md` for prose: first symmetric topology
(every peer is both client and server), discovery as gossip bootstrap,
catalogs as distributed indexes, chunked transfer as progressive
verification, content addressing decouples identity from location.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Distributed Knowledge Assembly

[research-brief @peers @query
    IN ORDER TO
        query each peer's local catalog concurrently
        download the best match from each via chunked transfer
        and assemble a composite brief once every transfer verifies

    concurrent
        @results <- @peers mapped concurrently to
                     [peer[catalog
                             {peer: @p, query: @query}]]
    join

    @transfers <- for @r in @results
        @content <- [peer[request-file
                            {from: @r:peer, hash: @r:best-match-hash}]]
            || -> #transfer-failed @r
        @content

    @transfers
        where every transfer verified is true
            -> [compose {brief: @transfers}]
        otherwise
            -> #partial-brief {available: @transfers,
                                missing: failed}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[peer
    new              {bootstrap-peers}
    discover         {peer}                      ;; gossip over neighbours
    publish-file     {peer, content}              ;; -> hash
    catalog          {peer, query}                 ;; local index only
    request-file     {from, hash}                  ;; chunked, verified
    chunk-hash       {content, offset, size}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: content hash `where` identity is location-independent

[peer[publish-file {peer: @p, content: @bytes}]
    @hash <- [hash[sha256 {bytes: @bytes}]]
    register (@hash → @bytes) in @p:local-store
    register @hash in @p:local-catalog
    return @hash
    IN ORDER TO
        make @hash usable as the file's identity across any peer
        so "file X" is fetchable from anyone who happens to have it
        not only from the peer who first published it
]

Same discipline G068 (thumbnail dedup), G100 (version store), and
every hash-indexed system uses — the file doesn't "live on Alice's
machine." It lives everywhere its hash does.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: chunked verify `where` each chunk is trusted separately

[peer[request-file {from: @src, hash: @h}]
    IN ORDER TO
        transfer @h in fixed-size chunks, verifying each independently
        so a corrupt chunk is re-requested without losing the whole file

    @manifest <- [@src[get-manifest {hash: @h}]]   ;; [chunk-hash, size]
    accumulate <- empty

    for @chunk-spec in @manifest:chunks
        @chunk <- [@src[get-chunk {hash: @chunk-spec:hash}]]
            where [hash[sha256 {bytes: @chunk}]] is equal to @chunk-spec:hash
                -> append @chunk to accumulate
            otherwise
                -> re-request this chunk (partial progress preserved)

    @assembled <- accumulate concatenated
    where [hash[sha256 {bytes: @assembled}]] is equal to @h
        -> return @assembled (file-level verification passed)
    otherwise
        -> #file-integrity-failed
]

Chunk is the atomic unit of trust. A corrupt chunk 7 of 10 means
re-request chunk 7 only. Same discipline large choreographies need:
if step 7 fails, re-execute step 7, not the whole dance.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @alice <- [peer[new {bootstrap-peers: []}]]
    @bob   <- [peer[new {bootstrap-peers: [@alice]}]]
    @carol <- [peer[new {bootstrap-peers: [@bob]}]]

    [peer[discover {peer: @carol}]]
    ;; carol learns about alice through bob's gossip

    @hash <- [peer[publish-file
                     {peer: @alice,
                      content: "vault research notes"}]]

    -> [peer[request-file {from: @alice, hash: @hash}]]
    ;; -> "vault research notes"  — chunked, each chunk verified
]
